= Project background and problem statement

== Organisational context and the starting point

NTG-agent-vnext is the QUOTA team's capstone contribution to NashTech's NTG Agent platform. The starting repository already provided a C\# conversational application with agent configuration, model-provider connections and supporting services. The team's task was to develop this foundation into a more configurable environment for document-grounded assistants, specialist delegation and interactive workflows. The original application and the capstone extensions therefore have different scopes: the report attributes the latter through the reviewed GitHub history rather than claiming authorship of the entire platform. @implementation @githubhistory

The completion plan describes four initial ambitions: a richer document-search pipeline, layered memory, planner-and-worker coordination, and an interface that could compose structured responses. It also records a subsequent concentration on LightRAG, assistants-as-tools, skills and role-based access. @completion These changes show that the project problem was broader than connecting a chat box to a model. It concerned how several assistants could share an application environment while retaining distinct responsibilities, knowledge and permissions.

For a document-grounded assistant, organisational information must be made available at the point of answering. RAG addresses part of this need by retrieving external evidence to condition generation. @rag However, retrieving a relevant document is only one stage of the user journey. The application must also determine which assistant a user can invoke, which documents that assistant searches, whether the selected provider configuration works, and how a multi-step task is presented to the user. NTG-agent-vnext addresses this combination of administration and runtime behaviour.

== Why the application is needed

Consider an illustrative use case derived from the project's requirements: a user asks a general assistant for help with a technical question that requires a specialist's document collection. The general assistant should be able to consult an appropriate inner agent when the user has access. The inner agent should retrieve from its own knowledge base and return an answer. If access is denied, the general assistant must not become an indirect route around the restriction. This scenario explains the requirement for both specialisation and permission-controlled delegation; it is not presented as a recorded sponsor interaction.

A second requirement-derived scenario concerns administration. An administrator adds a model endpoint, fetches the models or deployments available to that endpoint, and enables a model for an agent. A model appearing in a catalogue does not establish that every optional request parameter will be accepted. The administrator therefore needs feedback about the selected configuration before enabling thinking mode. GitHub history makes this need concrete: provider routing was revised in PR 281, while PR 287 replaced a curated thinking-capability list with a live request probe. @githubhistory

A third scenario concerns task interaction. A user may need to provide several fields, compare options and review a structured result. Repeatedly exchanging these values as free text can leave the application without an explicit representation of the current step or selected values. The project's skill templates and streamed UI events supply that representation. The included travel-planning and ticket-booking packages illustrate the interaction pattern with synthetic demonstration data; they are not evidence of real reservations or completed business transactions.

