== Problem statement and project objectives

The project addresses the following problem: *how can an existing .NET agent application support configurable, document-grounded specialist workflows while preserving caller permissions and maintaining consistent behaviour across model providers and user interfaces?* The difficulty lies in connecting these requirements. A knowledge boundary must survive delegation; a provider option must correspond to a usable request; and a task instruction must reach an interface capable of rendering its intended interaction.

Five objectives make the problem assessable. First, each knowledge-enabled agent should have a defined retrieval context and a document lifecycle that distinguishes upload from indexing readiness. Second, the runtime should expose and invoke specialist agents according to the caller's permissions. Third, administrators should manage available models and test optional thinking behaviour through the runtime request path. Fourth, skills and structured UI events should support reusable task flows. Fifth, the project should leave a reproducible setup process and evidence with which these capabilities can be evaluated.

These objectives also define boundaries. The project does not develop a new foundation model or retrieval algorithm. It adopts existing frameworks and protocols, then contributes the integration, configuration and lifecycle logic needed in this application. The retained design does not implement the completion plan's proposed many-to-many shared knowledge spaces, and removal of the earlier long-term memory backend is a documented scope decision. Likewise, demonstration skills are not integrations with live booking or enterprise transaction systems. @completion @githubhistory

== Constraints and significance

The work operates within an existing .NET application, a capstone delivery schedule and externally provided model services. Per-agent knowledge services introduce container and storage coordination, while the conversational frontend adds another process and protocol boundary. NashTech's infrastructure support and the university's Claude API allocation helped the team develop and test within these constraints. The reported setup friction also made local reproducibility a practical requirement rather than an incidental documentation task.

The project's significance is its treatment of the assistant as an administered application. The administrator defines the available capabilities; the runtime applies those choices during delegation and retrieval; and the client renders supported interactions. This is useful beyond a single demonstration because future assistants can reuse the same configuration and integration mechanisms while differing in their documents, permitted users and task guidance. The claim is a reusable engineering approach, not established superiority over every alternative platform.

The completion plan provides measurable targets for retrieval, answer quality, ingestion, response latency, availability and cost. @completion GitHub integration records establish when selected capabilities entered main, while the supplied retrieval trials establish a narrower set of observed outcomes. The report uses both forms of evidence: implementation and contribution records explain what was delivered; experimental results examine how the tested retrieval configuration behaved. Section 3 derives the design questions from prior work, Section 4 explains the solution and its development, and Section 5 assesses the available results against the retained objectives.
